  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Student Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Now that you have written equations from tables, it is time to write systems of
        equations from graphs.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <emphasis>Part 1: Writing a System of Equations from a Given Graph </emphasis>
     </para>
      <para id="para-00003">For questions 1 – 8, use the given graph:</para>
      
        <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_06_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      

        <exercise id="_0a49b738-8436-497d-8638-18fa613b6ced" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">For the green dashed line what is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Correct! 9</para>
            <para id="para-00006">The correct answer is 9.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ec23dfc4-4e5e-4dfc-b5a8-854b9fbd123c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">For the green dashed line what is the slope?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00008">Correct! -1</para>
            <para id="para-00009">The correct answer is -1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">What is the equation of the green dashed line in slope-intercept form?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00012">\(y= -x+9\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

        <exercise id="adafd334-fdb7-43a1-aa7d-69c9835cde5a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">

            <para id="para-00013">For the orange solid line what is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">

            <para id="para-00014">Nice try! The correct answer is 5.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e5b6a568-4cef-49f8-a35b-df8ac3233ff4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">

            <para id="para-00015">For the orange solid line what is the slope?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">

            <para id="para-00016">The correct answer is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00017">What is the equation of the orange solid line in slope-intercept form?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00019">\(y = 3x +5\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00020">Write the two equations as a system of linear equations.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">\(y= -x+9\)</para>
          <para id="para-00023">\(y = 3x +5\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00024">What is the solution of the system of equations?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00026">\((1, 8)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00027">
        <emphasis>Part 2: Use a Graph to Solve a Given System</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para id="para-00028">Now that you wrote a system of equations from a given graph, how can you work backward
        and graph a given system of equations by hand? Discuss this with a partner.</para>
      <para id="para-00029">For questions 1 - 2, use this system of equations:</para>
      <para id="para-00030">\(y=4x-4\)</para>
      <para id="para-00031">\(y= -2x+8\)</para>
      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <!--QUESTION
            IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para id="para-00032">On the same coordinate plane, graph \(y = 4x -4\) and \(y = -2x +8\) by hand.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00034">Compare your answer:</para>
            
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_06_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00035">What is the solution to the system of equations?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00036">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00037">\((2, 4)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00038">For numbers 3 and 4, consider the system of equations:</para>
      <para id="para-00039">\(y = - \frac{3}{4}x+5\)</para>
      <para id="para-00040">\(y=\frac{1}{2}x-5\)</para>
      <!--Q#3-->

        <exercise id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <!--QUESTION
            IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para id="para-00041">On the same coordinate plane, graph the system of equations by hand.</para>
            <para id="para-00042">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00043">Compare your answer:</para>
            
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_06_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00044">What is the solution to the system of equations?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">
          <para id="para-00045">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00046">\((8, -1)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Graphing a System of Equations</title>
      <para id="para-00047">Watch the following video to learn more about writing systems of equations from graphs.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/RQHhdqkJEo0?si=lGR1PpEOg-oREXv2;&amp;rel=0"/>
      </media>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
      will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->

      <!--Start
      interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
      have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
      button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
    </section>
    
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00013">
          <para id="para-00048">Graph the following system of equations on a coordinate plane:</para>
          <para id="para-00049">\(y=2x +5\)</para>
          <para id="para-00050">\(4x-2y =10\)</para>
          <para id="para-00051">Find the solution.</para>
          <para id="para-00052">(Hint: For the second equation, solve for \(y\) first.)</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00013">
          <para id="para-00053">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00054">Infinitely many solutions. When the second equation is solved for \(y\), it becomes
            \(-2y =-4x=10\) then \(y = 2x +5\). Both equations are the same. So, on the graph, one
            line is on top of the other and all solutions are possible.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
    </section>
  </section>

  <section id="sect-00005">
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00055">Which of the following systems of equations is graphed:</para>
      
        <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_07_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      
