  </section>

  <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Additional Resources</title>

    <section id="sect-00007">
      <title>Solving Systems by Graphing</title>
      <para id="para-00056">The graph of a linear equation is a line. Each point on the line is a solution to the
        equation. For a system of two equations, we will graph two lines. Then we can see all the
        points that are solutions to each equation. And, by finding what the lines have in common,
        we’ll find the solution to the system.</para>
      <para id="para-00057">Most linear equations in one variable have one solution, but we saw that some equations,
        called contradictions, have no solutions and for other equations, called identities, all
        numbers are solutions.</para>
      <para id="para-00058">
        
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00059">
        <emphasis>Part 1:Writing a System of Equations from a Given Graph</emphasis>
     </para>
      <note id="note-00002">
        <para id="para-00060">How to write a system of linear equations from graphs.</para>
        <para id="para-00061"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first line.</para>
        <para id="para-00062"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second line.</para>
        <para id="para-00063"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis>
        - Write the two equations for a system of equations.</para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00064">
        <emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para id="para-00065">Write a system of equations from the graph.</para>
      <para id="para-00066">
        
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00067"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
        \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}r}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
        \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\mathrm{\style{color:red}o}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
        line.</para>
      <para id="para-00068">\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(0-(-3))}{(6-0)}=\frac36=\frac12\)</para>
      <para id="para-00069">\(y\)-intercept = \((0, -3)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00070"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
        \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{blue}}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{solid}}\) line.</para>
      <para id="para-00071">\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(3-(-3))}{(3-0)}=\frac63=\frac21\)</para>
      <para id="para-00072">\(y\)-intercept = \((0, -3)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00073"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the two equations for a system of equations.</para>
      <para id="para-00074">\(y = mx+b\)</para>
      <para id="para-00075">
        \({\style{color:red}y} {\style{color:red}=}\frac
        {\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}3}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00076">\({\style{color:blue}y} {\style{color:blue}=}
        {\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}x}{\style{color:blue}-}{\style{color:blue}3}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00077">
        <emphasis>Part 2:Use a Graph to Solve a Given System</emphasis>
     </para>
      <note id="note-00003">
        <para id="para-00078">How to solve a system of linear equations by graphing.</para>
        <para id="para-00079"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first equation.</para>
        <para id="para-00080"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second equation.</para>
        <para id="para-00081"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis>
        - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular coordinate system.</para>
        <para id="para-00082"><emphasis>Step
        4</emphasis> - Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00083">If the lines intersect, estimate the point of intersection. Check to make sure it is a
        solution to both equations. This is the solution to the system.</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>If the lines are parallel, the system has no solution.</item>
        <item>If the lines are the same, the system has an infinite number of solutions.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00084">
        <emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para id="para-00085">Solve the system by graphing:</para>
      <para id="para-00086">\(y = 2x+1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00087">\(y =4x-1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00088"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and /(y/)-intercept of the first equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00089">In \(y=2x+1\), \(m=2\), \(b=1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00090"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00091">In \(y=4x-1\), \(m=4\), \(b= -1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00092"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular coordinate
        system.</para>
      <para id="para-00093">
        
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_03.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00094"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
      <para id="para-00095">The point where the lines intersect is:</para>
      <para id="para-00096">\((1, 3)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00097">Check to make sure the estimated point, \((1, 3)\) solves the system by substituting the
        values for \(x\) and \(y\) into both equations.</para>
      <para id="para-00098">Check the first equation: \(𝑦=2𝑥+1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00099">\(x = 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00100">\(y = 3\)</para>
      <para id="para-00101">\(y = 2x + 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00102">\((3) = 2(1) + 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00103">\(3 = 2 + 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00104">\(3 = 3\)</para>
      <para id="para-00105">So, \((1, 3)\) solves \(y = 2x + 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00106">Check the second equation: \(𝑦=4𝑥−1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00107">\(y = 4x - 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00108">\((3) = 4(1) - 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00109">\(3 = 4 - 1\)</para>
      <para id="para-00110">\(3 = 3\)</para>
      <para id="para-00111">So, \((1, 3)\) solves \(y = 4x - 1\)</para>

      <note class="try look-at-me" id="note-00004">
      <title>Try It: Solving Systems by Graphing</title>
      <!--Q#1-->
        <exercise id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00014">
            <para id="para-00112">Write a system of equations from the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00113">
              
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_04.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para id="para-00114">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">
            <para id="para-00115">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00116">Here is how to solve the system by graphing:</para>
            <para id="para-00117"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
              \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}r}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
              \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\mathrm{\style{color:red}o}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
              line.</para>
            <para id="para-00118">\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac
              {(0-(1))}{(2-0)}=\frac{-1}{2}=\frac{-1}{2}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00119"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
              \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{blue}}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{solid}}\) line.</para>
            <para id="para-00120">\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(1-(-3))}{(1-0)}=\frac{4}{1}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00121"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the two equations for a system of equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00122">
              \({\style{color:red}y} {\style{color:red}=}\frac
              {{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}1}}{{\style{color:red}2}}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}+}{\style{color:red}1}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00123">\({\style{color:blue}y} {\style{color:blue}=}
              {\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}x}{\style{color:blue}-}{\style{color:blue}3}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

        <exercise id="exer-00011">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            <para id="para-00124">Solve the system by graphing:</para>
            <para id="para-00125">\(y=3x+3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00126">\(y= -x+7\)</para>
            <para id="para-00127">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00128">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00129">Here is how to solve the system by graphing:</para>
            <para id="para-00130"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first
              equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00131">In \(y=3x+3\), \(m=3\), \(b=3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00132"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second
              equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00133">In \(y= -x+7\), \(m=-1\), \(b= 7\)</para>
            <para id="para-00134"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular
              coordinate system.</para>
            <para id="para-00135">
              
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_05.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
            <para id="para-00136"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00137">\((1, 6)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </section>  
